        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): handle ripgrep stdout/stderr stream errors in grep tool",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe built-in `grep` tool pipes ripgrep's stdout into a `readline.Interface` and attaches a `\"data\"` listener to stderr, but neither raw stream has an `\"error\"` listener. If either stream emits an error, readline's internal handler re-throws it as an unhandled exception and crashes the agent tool.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdded explicit `\"error\"` handlers on `child.stdout` and `child.stderr` that close the readline interface, remove the abort listener, and reject the tool promise with the stream error. A no-op `rl.on(\"error\", …)` is added so readline does not re-throw the input stream error after our handler has settled the promise.\n\n## User Impact\n\nGrep tool calls no longer crash on stdout/stderr stream errors; they return a failed result instead.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRegression test:\n\n```bash\npnpm test src/agents/sessions/tools/grep.stream-errors.test.ts\n```\n\n```\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\n     Tests  2 passed (2)\n```\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n```bash\nnode_modules/.bin/tsx scripts/proof/grep-tool-stream-errors.mts\n```\n\n```\n=== Proof: grep tool stream error handling ===\n\nPASS (stdout): stream error rejected with \"ripgrep stdout error: stdout EPIPE\"\nPASS (stderr): stream error rejected with \"ripgrep stderr error: stderr EPIPE\"\n```\n",
        "branch": "fix/grep-tool-stream-errors",
